<Project>
  <!--The compiler reads the XML doc comments (#594). Until this file no project set GenerateDocumentationFile,
      so a cref to a removed member, a <param> for a parameter that no longer exists or a doc comment left on
      nothing compiled without a word - BestPractices.md section 8 calls a stale <param> "a compiler-visible
      lie", and nothing was looking. With it on, CS1574/CS0419 (a cref that does not resolve or is ambiguous),
      CS1570 (malformed XML), CS1572/CS1571/CS1734 (a param or paramref that does not match the signature) and
      CS1587 (a doc comment on no member) are ordinary warnings every build and build.yml print.

      Two are suppressed, deliberately:
      - CS1591, a public member with no doc comment at all. Most members here carry none by choice; the
        comments are for the why, not a doc comment per accessor.
      - CS1573, a method documenting some of its parameters but not all. The codebase documents the parameter
        whose meaning is not obvious from its name and leaves the rest, which is a style, not a defect.

      A project that sets NoWarn of its own must write it as "$(NoWarn);..." - a bare value replaces this one
      and every missing doc comment in that project comes back as a warning.

      VectorBoundary is a Roslyn analyzer the compiler loads, not a library anyone reads the docs of, so it is
      left out. The documentation files are not published: the Game's release folder has no use for them.-->
  <PropertyGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' != 'VectorBoundary'">
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CS1591;CS1573</NoWarn>
    <PublishDocumentationFile>false</PublishDocumentationFile>
    <PublishReferencesDocumentationFiles>false</PublishReferencesDocumentationFiles>
  </PropertyGroup>
</Project>
